  <result aggregation-status="1" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Annual Output Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator ascending="0" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output Indicator</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">TBD</narrative>
    </description>
    <baseline year="2022">
     <comment>
      <narrative xml:lang="EN">TBD</narrative>
     </comment>
    </baseline>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2022-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2022-12-31"></period-end>
     <target>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">Outcome 1 &#x2013; A community-oriented response to essential human security and justice needs enhances civilian protection particularly in hot spot areas for survivors of human rights violations, particularly women and children and at-risk and vulnerable groups, and mitigates conflict escalation

Outcome 2 &#x2013; People in Sudan and communities partake in and advance the peace and reform processes, and key rule of law and human rights institutions at national and state levels are capacitated to implement gender-sensitive legal and institutional reforms based on the peace and political processes and related agreements, in line with international human rights and rule of law standards and norms

Outcome 3 &#x2013; An environment conducive to peaceful resolution of conflicts, accountability and redress for human rights violations, protection of civilians and durable solutions is progressively expanded through the operationalization of key nationally-owned legal and institutional reforms and rule of law strategic priorities, a victim-centered transitional process and extension of gender-responsive, transparent and accountable justice and security institutions</narrative>
      </comment>
     </target>
     <actual>
      <comment>
       <narrative xml:lang="EN">- Joint assessment of the criminal justice system response to SGBV and CRSV in Sudan - Geographical areas: Khartoum, Darfur and South Kordofan and Blue Nile 
- Enhanced evidence collection and medico-legal support 
- Sensitization sessions for IDPs in abou shouk IDP camp, El Fasher and at least three other camps on SGBV /CRSV accountability 
- Compilation of obstacles/issues faced in providing legal support to CRSV victims by a selected legel aid provider 
- Support to bar association on strategic litigation
- Ten-days Training of Trainers (ToT) for SPF and prosecutors on Sexual and Gender Based Violence 
and Conflict Related Sexual Violence
- Two - Five-day training for Sudanese Police Officers in Family and Child Protection Units and/or at Gender Desks as well as for prosecutors in topics of Sexual and Gender Based Violence/ Conflict-Related Sexual Violence, investigation, age-appropriate approach, and basics of Community-oriented Policing
- Strong platform of women judges to further enhance the resolution of SGBV/CRSV cases 
- Establish a national network for criminal justice and re-establish state level criminal justice forums
- Training of newly recruited field UNVs and Gender Team on SGBV and CRSV response/mitigation implementation
- Establish an expert advisory group from the diaspora to the UN and by extension government authorities that would assist on matters related to legal and policy change in combatting conflict related sexual violence. 
- A one-day seminar on sexual assault medical examination and documentation for use as evidence in courts for medical doctors, judicial and law enforcement authorities and lawyers dealing with SGBV in Darfur 
- One three-day workshop in El Fasher by international pathologist for medics, lawyers, prosecutors, judges, police investigators on forensic medical examination and documentation of SGBV cases with the purpose to use as evidence in courts
A three -day workshop in Khartoum for human rights lawyers from all regions on investigation and prosecution of SGBV focusing on rape cases</narrative>
      </comment>
     </actual>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
